00:00:00.080 --> 00:00:01.910 align:start position:0%
 
hey everyone it's sarah register nurse  rn.com and in this video i'm going to  talk about some things that you can do  if you miss a vein whenever you're  starting an iv  missing a vein whenever you're starting  an iv is something that you just don't  want to do whether you're a new nurse or  a seasoned nurse but it's something that  unfortunately  does happen even for the most seasoned  nurse there there will be times where  they miss  veins so you know as a nurse you don't  want to miss that vein because  usually whenever you're having to start  an iv on a patient it's for a reason  you've got to give a medication that  needs to really be given right then  and there or if you're the patient you  don't want to have to be stuck  with a needle over and over so first  whenever you do  miss a vein you want to make sure that  you have a good reaction to it  because you don't want to have one of  the two extremes like one extreme would  be  that you blame the patient for why you  miss the iv  like telling the patient oh man you have  horrible veins  and i can't get i don't know how anyone  really gets an iv on you  that could really offend the patient and  make them feel bad about themselves  or um you are just overly apologetic  and you're like i'm just so sorry i'm so  sorry i'm just really not good at this  i'm new here and just keep apologizing  instead you want to be tactful with how  you respond  for instance you can say okay this  didn't work over here so is it okay if i  look in this other arm  and one thing i do before i actually  start an iv on a patient  is that number one i ask them if it's  okay if i start an iv on them and  explain what an iv is if they're not  familiar with it  and then i ask them what vein i can use  on their arm because patients know their  body so much better than we do  and a lot of times patients will tell me  hey they always get an iv  over here in this arm and this is where  i prefer and nine times out of ten i'll  go there there's an awesome vein  and i'm able to stick it first try and  i'm glad i listened to the patient  and another thing to keep in mind if you  do miss is to always  ask the patient's permission if you can  attempt to stick them again  because some patients i have found they  will actually tell you no  i don't want any more attempts i'm not  having an iv  and then also you want to look at your  hospital's protocols  because many times these protocols will  dictate how much  an individual nurse can attempt to stick  a patient for an iv  and sometimes it's limited to two so you  always want to be familiar with that and  the next thing  is that you have to maintain confidence  especially whenever you miss  after that first attempt so you know  whenever you do miss after that first  attempt your confidence is already like  deflating and you're thinking yourself  oh no  i hope i really get this especially if  you have a patient  who is really putting the squeeze on you  and they're saying okay you're not  trying anymore after this  or you have family members who are  really just like you know you're not  sticking them anymore  and i actually have a true story about  that whenever i first started out in  nursing  had this sweet patient she was about in  her 90s  and here i am new to nursing and at that  time i wasn't that great at ivs but i  was trying  and i go into the patient's room need to  start an iv and her son who is a little  bit older  comes up to me he didn't even say hi or  anything he was like  you get one stick i was like okay  and i was thinking okay i hope i really  get this and  so i go to start it and he's like you  know they're at the bedside like  watching me  like a hawk making sure i'm doing  everything right and guess what  i miss and um i felt about this big  and just wanted to die really but  anyways  i had to go get another nurse who was  seasoned and she came in  and tried and unfortunately she missed  the first time but she was able to talk  the sun  into allowing her to try again and she  got it on her second attempt  so you really have to watch that and you  have to try to maintain your confidence  whenever  you know things are making you feel like  oh no i don't know if i can get this  another strategy you can do if you do  miss that vein on that first try  is you really want to investigate those  arms very carefully  because the next vein you choose you  want it to be something that you know is  going to work  because unfortunately sometimes you may  not be able to find anything  in the anacubital fossa in the forearm  or  on the dorsum of the hand and you may  have to go  through some non-traditional routes but  sometimes whenever you have to do that  you have to get the doctor's  permission in order to do that and i  have seen that done  like for instance i've seen ivs up here  in the chest i've also seen them on the  dorsum of the foot  one time i had a patient who was a very  heavy drug user  and they had absolutely nothing in their  arms so we had to go  on the top of the foot and another thing  you know if you are  missing veins a lot you need to step  back and ask yourself okay why am i  missing these veins what is going  wrong and some things that can go wrong  is that  you're just not finding a vein to use  that is actually able to hold that gauge  you're using so maybe  the gauge of iv cannula you're using is  way too big for the vein you selected  also you want to get familiar with  anatomy of the arms and where  those large veins veins set and be able  to know how to feel for them what they  feel like they have this  springy squishy feel to them another  thing is that  are you hitting the veins so you're  getting the blood in the chamber  but whenever you go to thread it in  you're having difficulty it's causing  the vein to blow well that could be  because you're not going  in far enough with that needle in order  to slide that cannula off into the vein  that's a big common problem that some  people have  also you may be putting too much  pressure on that vein with the  tourniquet  maybe you shouldn't even use a  tourniquet or if you do use one put it  on there very  lightly just to create pressure because  it's like the same concept with the  balloon  if you have that vein bulging out way  too much and you take that sharp needle  and hit it  all that pressure on that vein is just  going to cause it to blow but let's say  the problem  that you're having for why you're  missing the veins is because you can't  actually  find the vein and get it up there so you  can stick it with a needle  well here are some things that i suggest  that you can do to help you with that  one thing is just to get familiar with  how to palpate a vein  practice filling it and the best person  you can use is really yourself get a  tourniquet put it on your arm  and just feel on your arm where these  veins are located and  how they feel next is to use gravity  gravity is your friend because it's  going to help  cause that blood to pull in those veins  help them pop up so you can find them so  use gravity  have the hand dangled down or the arm  dangle down at the side  and apply a tourniquet as needed and you  can also have the patient  pump their hand but let's say you're  going to be collecting lab values  out of this iv you want to avoid doing  this because it could throw off some of  the lab values that you're collecting  and then another thing you can do is if  you have access to a vein finder or a  vein light these are really cool tools  that  are non-invasive you can put them on the  skin and it'll help  light up where that vein is located or  you can get ultrasound to help  you out with this it really varies on  what you have access to  in your hospital but i have had  ultrasound for patients who are really  hard to stick  they will come and they will actually  ultrasound the vein and they will  actually stick the vein and get the iv  and really another simple thing you can  do is you can use  heat if you have a blanket warmer on  your unit you can get some  warm blankets put those on the arm  especially if your patient's cold  and you can even notice this on yourself  whenever you're cold your veins will  shrink down  or you can get one of those warm  compresses and put those on the veins  just to help expand those out  and it may be that your patient just  literally has nothing to choose from so  they may be a candidate for a central  line but you would want to check with  the physician on that  and lastly you know if you find yourself  being in that situation where you're  just missing veins  all the time and you're never getting  them don't think that this is just a  skill that you're never going to get  because this skill takes lots of  practice unless you're one of those  people who came out of nursing school  just being like naturally awesome at  starting ivs most people it takes lots  of practice  and for me when i write a new nurse this  took practice  and i know it can be difficult whenever  you are missing all the time and maybe a  patient  has said something to you which squishes  your confidence even more  maybe a co-worker is giving you a hard  time about it but whatever it is  just push that out of your mind and just  say okay i'm going to keep practicing  i'm not going to give up because one of  the worst things you can do  is just quit trying and just keep asking  people to start your ivs for you  because there's going to be times when  this patient needs this iv  now because the iv blue you have some  fluids or antibiotics to run you need  the iv and you go out to look for  someone to help you and it's like a  ghost town you can't find anyone  and it comes down to you and this is  really a skill set that you have to have  so what you want to do is you just want  to practice take every opportunity you  can to practice and i know whenever  you are first starting out the skill  seems so complicated  because you have to first find the vein  then you have to stick the vein  and then you have to like have limited  amount of hands to be able to connect to  the extension set to flush it to  occlude it to secure it down and it  seems so  just difficult but over time when you do  this over and over you're gonna get it  and before you know it people may be  coming to you to get you to start their  iv so don't give up  okay so that wraps up this video and if  you'd like to watch more videos like  this you can access the link in the  youtube description below

